{"module":"veterans","voice":"female","duration":182.3,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Black veterans: fighting for a country that fought them","start":0.0,"end":3.12,"words":[[0.05,0.33],[0.33,0.97],[1.22,1.64],[1.64,1.78],[1.78,1.92],[1.92,2.35],[2.35,2.56],[2.56,2.77],[2.77,2.98]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Black Americans have served in every U.S. war.","start":3.92,"end":7.03,"words":[[3.98,4.25],[4.25,4.85],[4.85,5.05],[5.05,5.31],[5.31,5.44],[5.44,5.71],[5.71,6.04],[6.37,6.57]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Many came home expecting the freedom they had fought for, and that expectation made them targets.","start":7.38,"end":13.26,"words":[[7.43,7.71],[7.71,7.99],[7.99,8.27],[8.27,8.89],[8.89,9.03],[9.03,9.45],[9.45,9.66],[9.66,9.87],[9.87,10.08],[10.08,10.29],[10.53,10.74],[10.74,10.95],[10.95,11.78],[11.78,12.06],[12.06,12.27],[12.27,12.75]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Revolution and Civil War","start":13.61,"end":15.79,"words":[[13.66,14.62],[14.62,14.94],[14.94,15.47],[15.47,15.68]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Crispus Attucks, a man of African and Native descent, was the first killed in the Boston Massacre (1770).","start":16.49,"end":23.57,"words":[[16.54,17.03],[17.03,17.44],[17.68,17.82],[17.82,18.03],[18.03,18.17],[18.17,18.65],[18.65,18.86],[18.86,19.28],[19.28,19.76],[20.0,20.21],[20.21,20.35],[20.35,20.63],[20.63,20.9],[20.9,21.04],[21.04,21.18],[21.18,21.6],[21.6,22.01],[22.01,23.12]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Thousands of Black soldiers fought for independence, while others joined the British, who promised freedom to people enslaved by rebels.","start":23.92,"end":32.59,"words":[[23.97,24.56],[24.56,24.71],[24.71,25.01],[25.01,25.6],[25.6,25.82],[25.82,25.97],[25.97,26.85],[27.11,27.4],[27.4,27.7],[27.7,28.14],[28.14,28.29],[28.29,28.81],[29.06,29.21],[29.21,29.73],[29.73,30.17],[30.17,30.32],[30.32,30.69],[30.69,31.28],[31.28,31.5],[31.5,32.02]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In the Civil War, about 179,000 Black men served in the Union Army.","start":32.94,"end":38.68,"words":[[32.98,33.12],[33.12,33.26],[33.26,33.61],[33.61,33.82],[34.06,34.41],[34.41,36.5],[36.5,36.78],[36.78,36.99],[36.99,37.27],[37.27,37.41],[37.41,37.55],[37.55,37.97],[37.97,38.24]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Confederates massacred surrendering Black soldiers at Fort Pillow in 1864.","start":39.03,"end":44.73,"words":[[39.05,39.9],[39.9,40.44],[40.44,41.21],[41.21,41.52],[41.52,42.13],[42.13,42.29],[42.29,42.52],[42.52,42.9],[42.9,43.06],[43.06,44.13]]},{"el":"h1","text":"World War I and the backlash","start":45.63,"end":47.82,"words":[[45.68,46.07],[46.07,46.27],[46.27,46.46],[46.46,46.76],[46.76,46.95],[46.95,47.54]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The 369th Infantry, the “Harlem Hellfighters,” spent more time in combat than almost any other American unit.","start":48.52,"end":57.29,"words":[[48.57,48.72],[48.72,50.55],[50.55,51.26],[51.54,51.7],[51.7,52.1],[52.1,52.89],[52.89,53.29],[53.29,53.45],[53.45,53.76],[53.76,53.92],[53.92,54.4],[54.4,54.64],[54.64,55.19],[55.19,55.43],[55.43,55.67],[55.67,56.3],[56.3,56.78]]},{"el":"s1","text":"At home, the Army hanged 19 Black soldiers of the 24th Infantry after the 1917 Houston riot, 13 of them in a single day without appeal; in 2023 the Army set aside the convictions of 110 of the soldiers.","start":57.64,"end":73.95,"words":[[57.7,57.85],[57.85,58.23],[58.5,58.65],[58.65,58.88],[58.88,59.18],[59.18,59.71],[59.71,60.02],[60.02,60.62],[60.62,60.78],[60.78,60.93],[60.93,61.61],[61.61,62.22],[62.22,62.52],[62.52,62.67],[62.67,63.81],[63.81,64.34],[64.34,64.8],[65.06,65.44],[65.44,65.6],[65.6,65.82],[65.82,65.98],[65.98,66.13],[66.13,66.58],[66.58,66.81],[66.81,67.27],[67.27,67.65],[67.91,68.06],[68.06,69.2],[69.2,69.35],[69.35,69.58],[69.58,69.81],[69.81,70.19],[70.19,70.34],[70.34,71.1],[71.1,71.25],[71.25,72.39],[72.39,72.54],[72.54,72.69],[72.69,73.38]]},{"el":"s1","text":"After the war, Black veterans were beaten and lynched, some in uniform, during the Red Summer of 1919 (EJI, Targeting Black Veterans).","start":74.3,"end":84.63,"words":[[74.35,74.67],[74.67,74.83],[74.83,75.07],[75.35,75.66],[75.66,76.3],[76.3,76.46],[76.46,76.85],[76.85,77.09],[77.09,77.65],[77.92,78.16],[78.16,78.32],[78.32,78.96],[79.23,79.79],[79.79,79.95],[79.95,80.19],[80.19,80.5],[80.5,80.66],[80.66,81.77],[81.77,82.17],[82.45,83.0],[83.0,83.32],[83.32,84.03]]},{"el":"h2","text":"World War II and “Double V”","start":85.53,"end":87.93,"words":[[85.58,85.95],[85.95,86.14],[86.14,86.9],[86.9,87.19],[87.19,87.66],[87.66,87.85]]},{"el":"s2","text":"More than a million Black Americans served in World War II, including the Tuskegee Airmen.","start":88.63,"end":94.64,"words":[[88.68,88.83],[88.83,89.05],[89.05,89.19],[89.19,89.64],[89.64,89.93],[89.93,90.59],[90.59,90.89],[90.89,91.04],[91.04,91.33],[91.33,91.48],[91.48,92.14],[92.4,92.99],[92.99,93.14],[93.14,93.73],[93.73,94.17]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Pittsburgh Courier's “Double V” campaign called for victory over fascism abroad and racism at home.","start":94.99,"end":101.27,"words":[[95.04,95.19],[95.19,95.72],[95.72,96.24],[96.24,96.62],[96.62,96.77],[96.77,97.29],[97.29,97.59],[97.59,97.74],[97.74,98.27],[98.27,98.57],[98.57,99.09],[99.09,99.46],[99.46,99.69],[99.69,100.29],[100.29,100.44],[100.44,100.81]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1944 an explosion at Port Chicago, California, killed 320 men, most of them Black sailors loading ammunition; 50 who refused to return to the same unsafe work were convicted of mutiny.","start":101.62,"end":116.74,"words":[[101.67,101.83],[101.83,102.85],[102.85,103.01],[103.01,103.8],[103.8,103.96],[103.96,104.19],[104.19,104.82],[105.1,105.88],[106.16,106.48],[106.48,107.89],[107.89,108.21],[108.48,108.88],[108.88,109.03],[109.03,109.27],[109.27,109.58],[109.58,110.06],[110.06,110.53],[110.53,111.32],[111.59,111.99],[111.99,112.14],[112.14,112.69],[112.69,112.85],[112.85,113.24],[113.24,113.4],[113.4,113.56],[113.56,113.87],[113.87,114.35],[114.35,114.58],[114.58,114.74],[114.74,115.45],[115.45,115.61],[115.61,116.24]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Navy exonerated them in 2024.","start":117.09,"end":119.93,"words":[[117.14,117.26],[117.26,117.57],[117.57,118.29],[118.29,118.48],[118.48,118.6],[118.6,119.51]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Isaac Woodard and desegregation","start":120.83,"end":123.12,"words":[[120.87,121.28],[121.28,121.68],[121.68,121.93],[121.93,122.98]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In February 1946, hours after his honorable discharge, Sergeant Isaac Woodard was pulled off a bus in Batesburg, South Carolina, and beaten by the police chief until he was blind.","start":123.82,"end":136.76,"words":[[123.87,124.02],[124.02,124.63],[124.63,125.84],[126.11,126.41],[126.41,126.72],[126.72,126.94],[126.94,127.55],[127.55,128.23],[128.5,129.03],[129.03,129.41],[129.41,129.79],[129.79,130.01],[130.01,130.32],[130.32,130.47],[130.47,130.62],[130.62,130.85],[130.85,131.0],[131.0,131.68],[131.95,132.25],[132.25,133.01],[133.28,133.5],[133.5,133.88],[133.88,134.11],[134.11,134.26],[134.26,134.64],[134.64,134.94],[134.94,135.32],[135.32,135.47],[135.47,135.7],[135.7,136.23]]},{"el":"s3","text":"An all-white jury acquitted the chief.","start":137.11,"end":139.59,"words":[[137.15,137.3],[137.3,137.72],[137.72,138.14],[138.14,138.63],[138.63,138.77],[138.77,139.12]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Outrage over the case helped move President Truman to order the desegregation of the armed forces in 1948 (Executive Order 9981).","start":139.94,"end":150.42,"words":[[140.0,140.59],[140.59,140.88],[140.88,141.03],[141.03,141.33],[141.33,141.7],[141.7,141.92],[141.92,142.58],[142.58,143.02],[143.02,143.17],[143.17,143.39],[143.39,143.54],[143.54,144.5],[144.5,144.65],[144.65,144.79],[144.79,145.02],[145.02,145.38],[145.38,145.53],[145.53,146.64],[146.64,147.38],[147.38,147.6],[147.6,149.96]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Many civil rights leaders, including Medgar Evers, were veterans; Tulsa's defenders in 1921 were veterans too.","start":150.77,"end":159.76,"words":[[150.82,151.14],[151.14,151.54],[151.54,151.94],[151.94,152.42],[152.7,153.34],[153.34,153.74],[153.74,154.14],[154.42,154.58],[154.58,155.3],[155.58,156.06],[156.06,156.7],[156.7,156.86],[156.86,158.14],[158.14,158.3],[158.3,158.94],[158.94,159.18]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Recognition delayed","start":160.66,"end":162.2,"words":[[160.71,161.53],[161.53,162.03]]},{"el":"s4","text":"No Black soldier received the Medal of Honor for World War II until 1997, when seven were finally awarded it after a review; only one, Vernon Baker, was still alive.","start":162.9,"end":175.77,"words":[[162.96,163.2],[163.2,163.52],[163.52,164.07],[164.07,164.54],[164.54,164.7],[164.7,165.09],[165.09,165.25],[165.25,165.49],[165.49,165.65],[165.65,165.96],[165.96,166.12],[166.12,166.75],[166.75,167.14],[167.14,168.64],[168.92,169.16],[169.16,169.55],[169.55,169.71],[169.71,170.26],[170.26,170.73],[170.73,170.89],[170.89,171.21],[171.21,171.37],[171.37,171.84],[172.11,172.51],[172.51,172.82],[173.1,173.5],[173.5,173.97],[174.24,174.48],[174.48,174.8],[174.8,175.27]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Black veterans were also largely shut out of the GI Bill's benefits (see the module on wealth).","start":176.12,"end":181.38,"words":[[176.18,176.45],[176.45,176.99],[176.99,177.13],[177.13,177.47],[177.47,177.88],[177.88,178.08],[178.08,178.28],[178.28,178.42],[178.42,178.56],[178.56,178.96],[178.96,179.24],[179.24,179.78],[179.78,179.92],[179.92,180.05],[180.05,180.46],[180.46,180.6],[180.6,180.94]]}]}